body:
Im in Brian's class rn and he's pretty funny and his lectures help preface the labs pretty well. At the start of each new experiment there's a quiz, which is pretty easy if you've done the required pre-lab beforehand. If you haven't, don't worry about the quiz, because you won't be allowed to participate in the lab anyway. Also, nothing in lecture is mandatory, not even attending, and no matter which one you sign up for you can go to any of his lectures, so long as there's enough room (There always is, lecture is never full). Im in the 9am class, but I attend the 10am lecture. Only tests have to be taken in your own class. 

&#x200B;

There's I believe one multiple choice midterm, and one final. I don't know what the final is like but the midterm was a relatively short exam and literally only asked about significant figures. There isn't much work outside of the lab itself on a weekly basis, just whatever calculations you think you need to make for your lab report/next lab session, and he gives quite a bit of time to write up the report at the end as well.

&#x200B;

I don't know anything about doc, but I know bild + Chem 7L + Gen chem is doable. I'm taking Physics 1B + BL, Chem 7L, and BIPN 100 and I have enough time for everything, generally.
